/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  ctrl17_step_reaction_curve  --  THE OPEN-LOOP STEP TEST, and nothing else.

  This is the experiment a control course runs before it tunes anything: the
  loop is OPEN (there is no PID in this file), the manipulated variable is
  driven through a declared staircase of steps, and the plant's response is
  written to trajectory.csv.  The identification that turns that record into a
  model -- gain, time constant, dead time -- lives in `system/propsDict` and is
  run by choupoProps AFTERWARDS, in this same directory:

      runCase    tutorials/ctrl/ctrl17_step_reaction_curve    # the experiment
      choupoProps tutorials/ctrl/ctrl17_step_reaction_curve   # the analysis

  THE EXPERIMENT AND ITS ANALYSIS ARE SEPARATE ON PURPOSE.  choupoCtrl owns the
  time loop; the props bench owns the regression.  Putting the fit inside the
  dynamic binary would give the project a second reason to integrate, and
  putting the integration inside the bench would give it a second time loop.
  The price is two commands; the refusal you get for forgetting the first one
  names the second.

  THE PLANT is ctrl01's: a 1 L jacketed CSTR, pure compA feed at 320 K and
  5e-5 kmol/s, a mildly exothermic first-order isomerisation compA -> compB,
  UA = 50 W/K.  It is the SAME plant ctrl01 controls with a PID, which is the
  point -- ctrl01 tunes by hand, this case identifies the model those settings
  should have come from.

  THE STAIRCASE: base 320 K, then +5, back, +20, back.  FOUR reaction curves
  from one run, and they are not four copies of one another --

    * the +5 K and the -5 K steps connect the SAME two steady states, so their
      steady-state gains must agree by construction.  They do, to 5e-4, which
      is a free consistency check on the whole chain (integration, sampling,
      regression);
    * the +/-20 K pair sits at a hotter operating point, and Arrhenius makes
      the plant's gain LARGER there.  It is about 48 % larger, and that number
      is the honest error bar on any single-step identification.  It is
      published as `K_spread_relative`;
    * the up-step and the down-step of the same size give different apparent
      DEAD TIMES (31.4 s against 18.8 s on the +/-20 pair).  Neither is wrong;
      the dead time of a plant that has none is an artefact of the curvature
      the surrogate is trying to absorb, and it moves with the curvature.

  WHY THERE IS NO CONTROLLER HERE.  A reaction curve is an OPEN-loop record: if
  a controller were acting, the response would be the closed loop's and the
  identified model would already contain the controller you were about to
  design.  ctrl02 and ctrl05 step a disturbance with the PID running, which is
  a different experiment (disturbance rejection); ctrl08 drives a PRBS with the
  PID running, which is a third.  This one has one job.

  The `Schedule` controller below is a controller only in the software sense --
  it reads nothing, decides nothing, and writes a declared value onto the
  jacket temperature at declared times.  That is what makes the record open
  loop, and it is also what puts the driven signal into trajectory.csv as its
  own column (`JacketStep.MV`), which is what the identification reads.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        reactor;
        type        dynamicCSTR;

        operation
        {
            UA           50.0;             // W/K   (jacket coupling)
            T_jacket     320.0 K;          // K     (the schedule overwrites it)
        }

        reaction    compA_to_compB;
    }
);

controllers
(
    {
        name         JacketStep;
        type         Schedule;

        actuator     { unit reactor;  mv T_jacket; }

        //  Each hold is 3000 s.  The FIRST one is not a step: it lets the
        //  reactor settle from the authored 0/ state, so that the first step
        //  is applied to a plant that is standing still.  The bench checks
        //  that (`preStepSteadyMetric`) and refuses if it was not.
        schedule
        (
            { time 0;      value 320.0; }   // settle
            { time 3000;   value 325.0; }   // +5 K
            { time 6000;   value 320.0; }   // -5 K   (back)
            { time 9000;   value 340.0; }   // +20 K
            { time 12000;  value 320.0; }   // -20 K  (back)
        );
    }
);
